{
  "abstract": "A cyclic construction of rational Diophantine septuples seeks a function on an elliptic curve with rational seven-torsion whose seven translates satisfy all pairwise square identities. We prove two obstructions to this architecture. First, a nonconstant source with only simple rational poles has pole degree at least eight, and degree eight forces exactly four poles on each of two torsion orbits. Second, when the complementary three-point zero supports are parallel arithmetic progressions, no source exists even for two of the three required shift identities. The orbit separation is any rational point outside the torsion subgroup; it need not have a rational half-point. The proofs combine residue signs and Abel relations with majority-contact divisors, propagation of reflection around an odd cycle, and Hermite reconstruction of forced square roots. The remaining compatibility equations are eliminated exactly. Independent arithmetic implementations recompute six resultants for the unequal-orbit case and two resultants of degrees 1740 and 1502 for the progression case. Denominator audits and direct exceptional-fiber calculations retain all rational parameter charts. Algebraic midpoint factor fields then exclude apparent exceptional solutions whose doubles are not admissible rational points. The supplement provides the complete inputs, exact checks and reproduction instructions. These results constrain a sufficient construction architecture; they neither exclude all equal pole supports nor settle the existence of isolated rational Diophantine septuples.",
